    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Diabetes mellitus is a complex group of diseases characterized by impaired glucose homeostasis due to insufficient insulin secretion by pancreatic islet beta cells. Diabetes occurs when the pancreatic beta cells are destroyed by autoimmune attack (type 1 diabetes) or when their function is impaired (type 2 diabetes). Therefore, intensive research efforts are focused on developing a therapeutic strategy to replace functional beta cells. Glucagon-producing pancreatic alpha cells have demonstrated the ability to express insulin and other beta cell genes in mice when endogenous beta cells are experimentally ablated. However, the extent to which such cellular changes occur in the context of human diabetes is unclear. Moreover, it is unknown which molecular mechanisms regulate the fate of adult human alpha cells. To determine the degree of pancreatic islet cell fate change in diabetes, we examined human type 1 and type 2 diabetic pancreas samples for alpha cells that lost the expression of hallmark alpha cell genes and acquired the expression of beta cell genes. In type 1 diabetic samples, we found a subset of cells that expressed both glucagon (GCG) and key beta cell genes and lost the expression of the alpha cell gene ARX. In type 2 diabetic samples, we found cells that co-expressed GCG and beta cell genes, as well as INS+ cells that lacked the expression of the beta cell gene NKX6.1. To create methods to dissect genetic mechanisms maintaining human alpha cell identity, we developed a cell-purification strategy to isolate human alpha cells, and a method to label human alpha cells for lineage tracing. My work reveals profound changes in alpha and beta cell fate in diabetic islets, and provides new techniques for isolating and lineage-tracing human alpha cells.",
